Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Claude Sonnet 5 now available on AWS - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced Claude Sonnet 5, Anthropic's latest and most capable Sonnet model, which offers top-tier intelligence at Sonnet pricing for coding, agents, and everyday professional work at scale. This model delivers strong performance across various tasks, including coding, where it navigates large codebases, lands multi-file changes, and carries debugging and refactoring tasks through to completion with fewer rounds of correction. For agents, it calls tools precisely, holds state across many steps, and recovers from errors to ensure more runs finish correctly the first time. For knowledge work, it builds spreadsheets, drafts documents, and turns unstructured material into structured analysis. Customers can access Claude Sonnet 5 through two methods: Amazon Bedrock and Claude Platform on AWS. Amazon Bedrock keeps data within AWS infrastructure and provides access to Claude Sonnet 5 through a unified service with AWS-managed features like Guardrails, Knowledge Bases, and regional data residency. Claude Platform on AWS offers direct access to Anthropic's native platform experience and capabilities via the AWS Console, allowing users to build, test, and deploy with the same APIs, features, and console experience as working with Anthropic directly, unified with AWS billing and authentication","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/claude-sonnet-5-now-available-on-aws/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a service for building generative AI applications and agents, now supports Global cross-Region inference for Anthropic's Claude Sonnet 4 model. This feature allows users to route inference requests to any supported commercial AWS Region for processing, optimizing resources, and increasing model throughput. Previously, users could only choose cross-region inference profiles tied to specific geographies, which automatically selected the optimal region within that geography. With the new Global cross-region inference profile, users can distribute traffic across multiple AWS Regions, enabling higher throughput for generative AI use cases that do not require a specific geography. The documentation provides information on increasing throughput with cross-region inference, supported regions and models for inference profiles, and steps to use an inference profile in model invocation. This feature is designed to help manage unplanned traffic bursts and optimize resources during peak usage times","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/09/amazon-bedrock-global-cross-region-inference-anthropic-claude-sonnet-4/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"caching-for-llm-inference","skill_description":"Sets up a shared L2 KV cache for self-hosted LLM inference on Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey, so GPU servers load prefill KV state over EFA RDMA instead of recomputing it. Checks fit, estimates savings with KVTrace, builds a business case, creates the cache, connects vLLM or SGLang (also as NVIDIA Dynamo or llm-d workers) through an LMCache server, tunes for cost or latency, and troubleshoots. Use when a builder serves open-weight models on GPUs (for example H100 or B200, on Amazon EC2 or Amazon EKS) for multi-turn chat, agents, RAG, or long contexts and asks about KV cache offloading, prefix cache sharing, prompt caching for self-hosted models, LMCache with Valkey, or lower TTFT or GPU cost. Not for Bedrock prompt caching, LLM response or semantic caching, or general application caching.\n\nServices: elasticache, ec2\nTasks: deploy, optimize, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, data-scientist, devops\nWorkload: ai-ml","skill_name":"caching-for-llm-inference"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"redshift-guide","skill_description":"Amazon Redshift is NOT PostgreSQL; corrects LLM mistakes; covers SQL, metadata, connectivity/auth, storage, query performance, and operations. Applies ONLY to Redshift (provisioned or Serverless). Pushes back on: CREATE INDEX, string_agg, pg_catalog, text type, SERIAL, stl_query. Triggers on: Redshift SQL, Redshift CREATE TABLE, Redshift COPY/UNLOAD, slow or suddenly slow Redshift query, EXPLAIN plan, queue wait, stale statistics/ANALYZE, result cache, Redshift permission denied, Redshift disk full, storage growth, snapshot/backup cost, ghost rows/bloat, VACUUM, spill to disk, distribution skew, Redshift system views, QUALIFY, PIVOT, MERGE, materialized views, Redshift Data API (Python/boto3), Redshift workload management (WLM), concurrency scaling, Redshift resize, Spectrum, awsdatacatalog, connection failures (SSL/TLS, ODBC/JDBC, connection limit), IAM/SAML federation, IAM Identity Center, datashares. Does NOT apply to: S3 policies, Athena, Glue/Iceberg outside Redshift, Aurora, RDS, DynamoDB.\n\nServices: redshift, redshift-serverless, redshift-data-api\nTasks: query, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, database-administrator, data-analyst\nWorkload: data-warehouse, analytics, etl\n*","skill_name":"redshift-guide"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check Sonnet 5 global profile pricing details"}

